// t-typ-mod-06: the Ref name fast path's "same definition?" test
// (TypeRef::cells_agree, ResolvedRef::same_def) accepts a different typedef
// of the same name, so a function throws a type it does not declare and the
// JIT then reads a value at the wrong type.
//
// A definition's inferred throws are re-scoped into its own scope
// (graphix-compiler/src/node/lambda.rs:1808, Type::scope_refs) and keep their
// filled cells: f's thrown P<i64, string> (f's block: (i64, string)) becomes a
// ref with g's scope and the name P. contains' Ref x Ref fast path
// (graphix-types/src/typ/contains.rs:549, union_identical's Ref arm at
// setops.rs:98) relates two refs by scope, name and cells_agree
// (graphix-types/src/typ/mod.rs:586-591). cells_agree answers true when
// either cell is empty (g's written P is never resolved before this
// comparison) or dead, and when both are filled it compares the definitions
// with Type::eq (same_def, mod.rs:402-405), under which any two open type
// variables are equal, so ('a, 'b) and ('b, 'a) are one definition.
//
// command: graphix --no-cache design/review-2026-10-05/repro/t-typ-mod-06.gx
//
// expected: refused at g: g's Error<ErrChain<P<i64, string>>> ((string, i64))
//   does not contain f's ((i64, string)), as it is refused when g writes
//   `throws Error<ErrChain<(string, i64)>>`.
// observed (HEAD c722befe, debug build): --check exit 0. The run aborts in
//   the fused str::len: "misaligned pointer dereference: address must be a
//   multiple of 0x8 but is 0x1" (arcstr), the i64 1 read as a string.
//   --no-fusion prints nothing; graphix-fuzz check aborts the same way. With
//   `got: [i64, null]`, `got <- n` and `got$ * 2 + 1` the JIT prints a heap
//   address (k = 280820668005313) and the node-walk an arith error parsing
//   "one".
// variants (all --check exit 0):
//   g's `type P<'a, 'b> = {x: 'a, y: 'b}` (any body, through the empty cell);
//   `type E = `A(string)` in f, `type E = `B(i64)` in g (arity 0).
//   Both cells filled, same_def alone: in g add `type T = Error<ErrChain<P<i64,
//   string>>>; let probe = |e: T| ((e.0).error); let fill: P<i64, string> =
//   probe(never());` and declare `throws T`: the swapped tuple is still
//   accepted and aborts the same way, the struct body is now refused.
let f = {
  type P<'a, 'b> = ('a, 'b);
  |x: i64| -> i64 throws Error<ErrChain<P<i64, string>>> select x {
    0 => error((1, "one"))?,
    n => n
  }
};
let g = {
  type P<'a, 'b> = ('b, 'a);
  |x: i64| -> i64 throws Error<ErrChain<P<i64, string>>> f(x)
};
let got: [string, null] = null;
let r = {
  catch(e) {
    let (s, n) = (e.0).error;
    got <- s
  };
  g(0)
};
let k = str::len(got$) + 1;
println("k = [k]");
sys::exit(sys::time::after_idle(duration:100.ms, 0))
